$DIR/main.p:20:2: cannot store a pointer into main's frame in memory that lives longer, which outlives main's frame
    a pointer into main's frame points into the object of a temporary error in
    main's frame. The error made at main.p:20 is a temporary: nothing keeps it,
    so its object is dropped at the end of the statement, and a view of the
    object may not outlive that statement. At the end of the statement, the
    temporary is dropped and its object freed, but memory that lives longer may
    be on the heap and live longer, so it would be left pointing at reclaimed
    memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value itself instead of a view of main's frame, or allocate it so
    that it lives as long as memory that lives longer.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
